12. The most important visual rule

I would use real Vietcotuong UI everywhere.

This is the main thing I would take from Onorca.

Its page repeatedly demonstrates the actual environment—workspaces, tasks, terminals, editors, and other product surfaces—instead of relying only on abstract marketing graphics.

For Vietcotuong, that means:

Don't use:

generic Xiangqi illustrations
random chess pieces
decorative gradients with no purpose
generic feature icons everywhere

Use:

real boards
real games
real move trees
real collections
real opening structures
real engine analysis
real workspace screenshots/components

Your product itself is visually interesting enough.

My final recommendation in one sentence
Design Vietcotuong's homepage as a guided entrance into a living Xiangqi environment, where the hero demonstrates the real product and the rest of the page progressively reveals the different ways users can explore it.

If this were my product, I would make the large interactive Vietcotuong workspace in the hero the centerpiece of the redesign. Everything else—the library, play app, CBL Browser, Game Workspace, Canvas Workspace, opening tools, and command panel—would then appear as natural paths branching out from that central experience.

That gives you one coherent story instead of a homepage that feels like a collection of unrelated features.

